FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/stdlib.rs:2050 — The nine internal `logging_*` names are registered in `BUILTIN_NAMES` (lines 2050–2058), so a bare call such as `logging_debug("x")` resolves as a builtin even though the logging section of `SPEC.md` says these are functions of the module and their bare spellings are not a second surface; `src/runtime.rs:3581` dispatches that exact name. This exposes an undocumented second spelling of every logging builtin. Fix: keep the module-internal names out of the bare registry (resolve dotted calls through `MODULE_MEMBERS`), or make the analyzer refuse a bare module-internal name, and add a test that `logging_debug("x")` is `Unknown function`. I traced the registry and dispatch but did not run the analyzer; a test calling the bare name would settle it.
2. [MINOR] src/logging.rs:237 — On non-Unix targets `write_stderr` uses `io::stderr().write_all` instead of the Unix direct-descriptor path that deliberately detects `EBADF`/`EPIPE`, so the documented catchable closed-stderr behavior is only implemented and tested on Unix (all the closed-stream tests are `#[cfg(unix)]`). Fix: use a fallible handle write that reports closed/broken descriptors on every platform, or document the platform limitation.
3. [MINOR] src/bytecode/vm.rs:4380 — The reset was added only to the free `run`; `BytecodeVm::run`, used by the one-shot compiler run at `src/bootstrap.rs:380`, still inherits the thread-local configuration, so the REPORT’s “Every program run starts from those defaults” is broader than the code. Fix: have the bootstrap run go through the resetting entry point (or reset explicitly) rather than relying on the REPL’s no-reset behavior.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
